---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="How Much Life Insurance Do You Actually Need?" description="Stop guessing. Use the DIME method to calculate the exact amount of life insurance your family needs.">
  <main class="container">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>How Much Life Insurance Do You Actually Need?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Written by the Engineering Team at Finance Hub</p>

      <p>Insurance salespeople will often throw out a random number like "$500,000" or "10x your salary." While those are decent rules of thumb, they don't account for your specific life. At Finance Hub, we prefer mathematical precision.</p>

      <h2>The DIME Method Explained</h2>
      <p>The DIME method is a highly respected calculation that covers the four major financial burdens your family would face if you passed away:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>D - Debt:</strong> Credit cards, student loans, and car payments.</li>
        <li><strong>I - Income:</strong> 10 years of your salary to replace your lost income.</li>
        <li><strong>M - Mortgage:</strong> The remaining balance on your home loan.</li>
        <li><strong>E - Education:</strong> The estimated cost of sending your children to college.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can plug your exact numbers into our free <a href="/tools/life-insurance-needs-calculator">Life Insurance Needs Calculator</a> to get your target coverage amount in seconds.</p>

      <h2>Term vs. Whole Life Insurance</h2>
      <p>As engineers, we look at the ROI. Whole life insurance is an investment product wrapped in insurance. It is incredibly expensive and often has poor returns. Term life insurance (e.g., a 20 or 30-year policy) is pure protection. It is vastly cheaper and allows you to invest the difference in index funds.</p>
      <p>For 95% of people, a simple Term Life policy that covers you until your kids are independent and your mortgage is paid off is the mathematically optimal choice.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
